Great topic 👍 — Virtual Threads are an advanced feature in Java (introduced in Java 19 as preview, stabilized in Java 21)
 to make concurrency lightweight.

🔹 What are Virtual Threads?

A virtual thread is a lightweight thread managed by the JVM (not directly by the OS).

Unlike platform threads (traditional Java threads), they don’t map 1-to-1 to OS threads.

Thousands (even millions) of virtual threads can run concurrently without heavy memory/CPU cost.

🔹 Why Virtual Threads?

Traditional threads are expensive → each consumes ~1MB stack memory.

Virtual threads use small memory and are scheduled by JVM, so they are cheap to create.

Great for apps with I/O bound tasks (like servers, HTTP requests, DB calls).

🔹 Example
public class VirtualThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Runnable task = () -> {
            System.out.println("Running in: " + Thread.currentThread());
        };

        // Create a virtual thread
        Thread vt = Thread.ofVirtual().start(task);

        vt.join();
    }
}


👉 Output (example):

Running in: VirtualThread[#21]/runnable@ForkJoinPool-1-worker-1

🔹 Executor with Virtual Threads
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    IntStream.range(1, 5).forEach(i ->
        executor.submit(() -> {
            Thread.sleep(1000);
            System.out.println("Task " + i + " in " + Thread.currentThread());
            return i;
        })
    );
}

🔹 Key Benefits

Create millions of concurrent tasks.

Simpler async code → looks synchronous but scales better.

Perfect for web servers, microservices, DB queries.

✅ Interview Short Answer:
“Virtual Threads are lightweight threads introduced in Java 19/21 that allow us to run millions of concurrent tasks efficiently.
 They reduce the overhead of traditional threads and are ideal for I/O-heavy applications.”